Here is an example JavaScript async function with error handling:

```javascript
async function fetchData(url) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
    }
    const data = await response.json();
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Fetch error:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

You can use this pattern to safely perform network requests.
